{"subjectId":"sql","id":"outer-joins","title":"LEFT, RIGHT & FULL OUTER JOIN","group":"Joins & Set Operations","order":27,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Outer joins keep rows that have <strong>no match</strong>, filling the missing side with <code>NULL</code>s. Use them whenever “no related rows” is a valid answer.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrow-bar-left\"></i>LEFT JOIN</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT d.name AS department, e.name AS employee\nFROM departments d\nLEFT JOIN employees e ON e.dept_id = d.dept_id\nORDER BY d.dept_id, e.name;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>department  | employee\n------------+---------\nEngineering | Asha\nEngineering | Karan\nEngineering | Meera\nEngineering | Ravi\nSales       | Arjun\nSales       | Neha\nSales       | Priya\nHR          | Sara\nHR          | Vikram\nMarketing   | NULL</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Marketing appears with a <code>NULL</code> employee: every left row survives. Counting per department must count a <em>column from the right side</em>:</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT d.name, COUNT(e.emp_id) AS headcount, COUNT(*) AS wrong_count\nFROM departments d\nLEFT JOIN employees e ON e.dept_id = d.dept_id\nGROUP BY d.dept_id, d.name\nORDER BY d.dept_id;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>name        | headcount | wrong_count\n------------+-----------+------------\nEngineering | 4         | 4\nSales       | 3         | 3\nHR          | 2         | 2\nMarketing   | 0         | 1</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p><code>COUNT(*)</code> counts the padded row, so Marketing shows 1. <code>COUNT(e.emp_id)</code> skips the <code>NULL</code> and correctly shows 0.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-horizontal\"></i>RIGHT and FULL OUTER JOIN</h3>\n<p><code>A RIGHT JOIN B</code> is <code>B LEFT JOIN A</code>. <code>FULL OUTER JOIN</code> keeps unmatched rows from <strong>both</strong> sides. SQLite supports both since 3.39 (2022); MySQL has <strong>no</strong> <code>FULL OUTER JOIN</code>.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT e.name AS employee, d.name AS department\nFROM employees e\nFULL OUTER JOIN departments d ON d.dept_id = e.dept_id\nORDER BY d.dept_id NULLS LAST, e.emp_id;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>employee | department\n---------+------------\nAsha     | Engineering\nRavi     | Engineering\nMeera    | Engineering\nKaran    | Engineering\nPriya    | Sales\nArjun    | Sales\nNeha     | Sales\nVikram   | HR\nSara     | HR\nNULL     | Marketing\nDev      | NULL</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>-- MySQL workaround for FULL OUTER JOIN: LEFT JOIN ∪ the right-only rows\nSELECT e.name AS employee, d.name AS department\nFROM employees e LEFT JOIN departments d ON d.dept_id = e.dept_id\nUNION ALL\nSELECT NULL, d.name\nFROM departments d\nWHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM employees e WHERE e.dept_id = d.dept_id)\nORDER BY department, employee;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>employee | department\n---------+------------\nDev      | NULL\nAsha     | Engineering\nKaran    | Engineering\nMeera    | Engineering\nRavi     | Engineering\nSara     | HR\nVikram   | HR\nNULL     | Marketing\nArjun    | Sales\nNeha     | Sales\nPriya    | Sales</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-alert-triangle\"></i>ON vs WHERE in an outer join</h3>\n<p><em>List every customer with their delivered orders.</em> The filter on the right table must go in <code>ON</code> — in <code>WHERE</code> it removes the <code>NULL</code> rows and silently turns the query into an inner join.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>-- filter in ON: all 5 customers kept\nSELECT c.name, o.order_id\nFROM customers c\nLEFT JOIN orders o ON o.customer_id = c.customer_id AND o.status = 'delivered'\nORDER BY c.customer_id, o.order_id;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>name   | order_id\n-------+---------\nAnanya | 101\nAnanya | 103\nRahul  | 102\nRahul  | 106\nSneha  | NULL\nImran  | NULL\nKavya  | NULL</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>-- filter in WHERE: Sneha, Imran and Kavya disappear\nSELECT c.name, o.order_id\nFROM customers c\nLEFT JOIN orders o ON o.customer_id = c.customer_id\nWHERE o.status = 'delivered'\nORDER BY c.customer_id, o.order_id;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>name   | order_id\n-------+---------\nAnanya | 101\nAnanya | 103\nRahul  | 102\nRahul  | 106</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>Rule: conditions on the <strong>optional</strong> (right) side of a <code>LEFT JOIN</code> go in <code>ON</code>; conditions on the <strong>preserved</strong> (left) side go in <code>WHERE</code>. The exception is deliberately testing for <code>NULL</code> (<code>WHERE o.order_id IS NULL</code>), which finds non-matching rows — an anti-join.</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is the difference between LEFT JOIN and INNER JOIN?</summary><div><p><code>INNER JOIN</code> returns only rows with a match on both sides. <code>LEFT JOIN</code> returns every row of the left table, with the right table's columns <code>NULL</code> where there is no match. The row count of a left join is always ≥ the left table's count (more if one left row matches several right rows).</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How do you emulate FULL OUTER JOIN in MySQL?</summary><div><p><code>LEFT JOIN</code> the two tables, then <code>UNION ALL</code> the rows of the right table that have no match (<code>RIGHT JOIN … WHERE left.key IS NULL</code> or <code>NOT EXISTS</code>). Plain <code>UNION</code> of a <code>LEFT</code> and a <code>RIGHT</code> join also works but de-duplicates, which can drop genuine duplicate rows.</p></div></details></section>"}